          // control points from the chord. `skew` is the term added on 2026-07-31: the distance
          // between the curve's mid-PARAMETER point B(0.5) and the chord's mid-POINT. Neither
          // subsumes the other, which is why the criterion takes the larger of the two.

new:
// Flatness measures control-point distance from the chord; skew measures the distance
          // between B(0.5) and the chord midpoint. Use the larger: either test can miss a curve
          // that the other detects.

old:
// The ticks are not this box's problem, and they are not the renderer's either:
          // `niceTicks` is one public copy of Heckbert's algorithm that five files each had
          // privately. The plot frame it landed on is readable too.

// Bar and point marks set `onClick`, `hoverColor` and `label`, which is exactly what
          // `Box.lines` looks for when it decides between the interactive SVG renderer and Skia's
          // (someSkiko.kt:37). So hovering and clicking work on a chart nobody configured —
          // by construction rather than by wiring.

// A plain triangle wave -- constant speed, every value of t given the same
                  // time. An earlier version cubed this about the centre to linger at t = 0.5,
                  // on the assumption that the middle was the interesting part. Captured frame
                  // by frame it is not: t = 0.5 is a clean closed ring, the tidiest and least
                  // ambiguous form in the whole sweep, and the shapes worth watching are the
                  // buckling ones either side of it at roughly 0.2-0.3 and 0.7-0.9. Easing
                  // towards the centre parks on the dullest frame. Slow everywhere beats clever.

// A fallback rather than a blank box: build the same shape with Field's own marching
              // cubes, so what Manifold adds shows by its absence. This mesh is watertight but NOT
              // a manifold you could subtract from.

// Deliberately no fenced code block in this string. A triple-backtick run anywhere in a
              // box's code — including in a comment like this one — makes the materialized document.md
              // wrap that property in a FOUR-backtick fence, and the completion corpus's document
              // parser only reads three, so the box silently drops out of tools/completion-corpus.
              // Inline code costs nothing and keeps it in.

// All ten spellings, all three clocks, side by side. `operations.yaml` in the
          // marf_demo document records these being tried one after another about five seconds
          // apart. Read the STAGES column: eight of the ten are one of three sequences.

// The one lossy step in the trip is the printing: `%.8f` is eight significant decimals and
              // binary32 needs nine to round-trip in general, so a fitted value can land one ulp away in the
              // source. Both outcomes have been seen and both are fine: `StackSLFitFigureTest`'s 64x64 fit
              // round-trips at exactly 0, and this box at 48x48 measured 5.96e-08, which is one ulp of a
              // Float near 1. That is why the guard allows 1e-4 rather than demanding zero.

new:
// Printing with %.8f keeps eight decimal places, not eight significant digits.
              // Its round-trip error depends on magnitude: a fitted Float may change in source.
              // This 48x48 example previously measured 5.96e-08; the 1e-4 guard checks the actual
              // error above instead of requiring an exact zero.

